# 2026-08-04, the safe-key check: crypto.zig 410 -> 494.
#
#   From the security review the megaplan has carried unticked since the docs
#   section was written -- "key file permissions, privilege drop, socket
#   permissions". A DKIM or ARC private key is a bearer credential for a domain,
#   and nothing in the suite looked at its mode before handing it to OpenSSL.
#   `securedkim-genkey` has created keys 0600 at `open` since D-8, but that only
#   covers keys this suite generated; one arriving from `openssl genrsa`, a
#   backup, or a config-management system at 0644 was loaded without comment.
#
#   About eighteen lines of that are code -- `KeyFileTrust`, `keyFileMode`,
#   `requireSafeKeyFile`, `KEY_PERMISSIONS_ADVICE`, and two lines inside
#   `loadRsaKeyFile`. The rest is comment, and the two long passages are the ones
#   a reader will otherwise have to reconstruct: why the check is a
#   misconfiguration guard rather than an anti-TOCTOU measure (an attacker who
#   can swap the file mid-load can equally write their own key there first), and
#   why there is no `RequireSafeKeys` override when opendkim ships one (the key is
#   re-read after the privilege drop, so the only arrangement that reloads is
#   0600 owned by the daemon user -- which is exactly what the check accepts, so
#   the flag could only ever be used to keep a key exposed).
#
#   ALL OF IT LANDS ON ONE SIDE OF THE SEAM THIS FILE ALREADY NAMED. The 2026-07-31
#   note above proposed splitting "what a key is and how it is admitted" from "the
#   operations", and every line added here is admission: a trust mode, a mode
#   reader, an admission check, and a branch in a loader. The split therefore got
#   both larger and better-motivated, not harder -- it now carries ~84 more lines
#   away from a file whose operations half is unchanged.
#
#   Still not taken here, for the reason the earlier note gives and which applies
#   unchanged: this is a security fix with its own regression tests, and burying
#   it inside a module split would make it harder to review and harder to revert.
#   The number to hit is set by the split, not by this entry.
#
# 2026-08-04: `loadRsaKeyFile` gained a THIRD required argument, `trust`.
#
#   Recorded because it is an API break across three repositories and nine call
#   sites, and the reason it is required rather than defaulted is the same reason
#   `min_bits` is: a new caller has to decide. The daemons and the two signing
#   CLIs pass `.require_safe`; `securedkim-testkey` and `securearc-testkey` pass
#   `.permit_any` and print the mode instead, because refusing to open a key is
#   the one thing that would stop a tool whose entire job is to report on it.
#
# 2026-08-05, stage 5.1: crypto.zig 494 -> 529, and it is a net removal.
#
#   `publicKeySpkiDer` and `publicKeySpkiBase64` -- the `i2d_PUBKEY` inverse of
#   `loadRsaPublicKeyDer`, which was already here. The export existed FOUR times
#   before this: `securedkim/src/testkey.zig`, `securearc/src/testkey.zig`,
#   `securedkim/src/genkey.zig`, and this file's own `testSpkiDer` helper. Each
